Done — the Stop hook is live and verified. Here's what I built and how it answers your "mtime isn't 100% accurate" concern.

## What's installed
- Helper: ~/.claude/hooks/ph_lo_stop_reminder.py
- Hook: a Stop entry in ~/.claude/settings.json (global; your existing UserPromptSubmit reminder preserved). Self-gating — silent in any cwd without response.history/prompt.history.

## The better-than-mtime detector (your research ask)
Instead of guessing from response.history's timestamp, the hook reads the session transcript (transcript_path on stdin) and checks whether the logging scripts actually ran in the current turn — it scans the assistant's Bash tool_use calls since the last real user prompt for logoutput.py/append_response_history and ph.py/append_prompt_history.

| | mtime guess (rejected) | transcript check (built) |
|---|---|---|
| "Already logged?" | inferred from file timestamp — fuzzy | verified — did the script run this turn? |
| Big false positive (nag after I did log) | happens | eliminated |
| Residual false positive (trivial turn) | yes | trimmed by a short-prompt filter (pwd/ok/好//cmd…) |
| Loop risk | n/a | guarded by stop_hook_active (nudges once, self-clears) |

## Verified (7 pipe-tests, all correct)
- both scripts ran → silent; logging missing + substantive → fires decision:block; trivial prompt → silent; loop-guard → silent; non-logging cwd → silent; malformed stdin → silent (never crashes).
- settings.json valid; Stop registered; UserPromptSubmit intact.

## One caveat (handoff)
Stop fires at turn-end, so I can't prove it live in-turn. The config watcher should pick up ~/.claude/settings.json (it existed at session start), but if the nudge doesn't appear when expected, open /hooks once (reloads config) or restart. You can review/disable it there anytime.

These live in ~/.claude/ (global config), outside the repo — so nothing to commit to the project.




